{
  "id": "brakes",
  "name": "Brakes",
  "summary": "The Gen 6 ZL1 / ZL1 1LE arrives with Brembo 6-piston front (390x36 mm two-piece rotors) and 4-piston rear (365x28 mm) calipers, a vacuum booster with tandem master cylinder, a four-channel ABS/StabiliTrak modulator and an electronic cable-puller park brake. A bare-shell race build normally removes the booster and factory ABS electronics in favor of a floor-mount dual-master pedal box with a cockpit-adjustable balance bar, optionally a Bosch Motorsport ABS kit, stainless -3 plumbing, race pads/fluid and full front brake ducting – or upgrades to AP Racing / Wilwood race calipers.",
  "overview": "How it works: pedal force is multiplied by the pedal ratio, split by the balance bar between a front and a rear master cylinder, converted to hydraulic pressure, optionally modulated by ABS, and delivered through hard lines and flex hoses to opposed-piston calipers that clamp vented iron rotors. On the factory car the vacuum booster adds force and the EBCM provides electronic brake-force distribution (there is no mechanical proportioning valve).\n\nKey decisions for a road-race Camaro: (1) keep or delete ABS – the factory EBCM/BPMV needs the factory CAN environment (ECM torque, steering angle, yaw), so with a stand-alone race ECU it is either replaced by a Bosch Motorsport M5/M7 kit or deleted; (2) caliper package – the factory ZL1 6-pots are strong but their dust boots cook on a full race car and pad volume is small (~15.8 mm), so Essex/AP Radi-CAL kits (25 mm pads, D62 heavy-duty option, 390 mm for high power) or Wilwood SX6R are common; (3) wheel size – 390 mm ZL1 rotors force 19 in wheels, while the Essex 372 mm kit fits behind 18 in ATS-V-size wheels, which opens up cheaper 18 in slicks; (4) cooling – a heavy car with 600+ hp must have rotor-eye ducting.\n\nRules: NASA and SCCA production-based classes generally allow brake upgrades and ABS changes within class-specific limits (check your class: some spec/production classes require OEM calipers or restrict ABS); all series require a functioning dual-circuit system, brake lights, and secure lines inside the cockpit (no fittings where the driver can be sprayed – route through bulkheads). Trans Am TA2-style rules mandate specific spec components. Always confirm with the current rulebook before buying.\n\nCommon failure points: boiled fluid (long pedal) from insufficient ducting or old fluid; cracked rotors; caliper boots/seals cooked; pad knock-back from worn hub bearings; flex hose chafe; loose bleeders or reused crush washers; torque-to-yield hat/caliper bolts reused; EPB shoes dragging; ABS sensor wiring melted near the caliper.",
  "buildNotes": [
    "Phase 1: fabricate pedal-box floor plate and drill firewall bulkhead holes for lines before paint.",
    "Phase 2: mount pedal box and masters, remote reservoirs, ABS unit (if used), and bend/clamp all hard lines (front via firewall bulkheads, rear down the floor) while the shell is empty.",
    "Phase 3–4: after suspension: install duct backing plates (or shields), hub bearings, rotors, calipers (bleeders up!), flex hoses with new crush washers, park brake (if kept).",
    "Pressure-test the hydraulic system before installing interior panels: hold pedal hard 60 s, no drop, no weeps.",
    "Phase 8: wire brake light/pressure sensors, ABS power/CAN, wheel speed sensors to PDM/logger.",
    "Phase 10: brake duct inlets in fascia/splitter and hose routing; check steering lock/bump clearance.",
    "Phase 11: fill with DOT 4 racing fluid, bleed RR-LR-RF-LF (inner then outer), set balance bar baseline, bed pads, verify bias with pressure sensors on track."
  ],
